{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000e2ff","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0adcf-af6f-7db0-8726-470499e8596b"},"sequence":4058,"recorded_at":1780531400008111,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"3b578cbb-cd1c-4970-859d-8c242cd48356","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"3b578cbb-cd1c-4970-859d-8c242cd48356","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"3b578cbb-cd1c-4970-859d-8c242cd48356"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0adee-6b38-74e3-9016-c618a2c822ee"},"task_id":"01a0adee-6b38-74e3-9016-c618a2c822ee","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0adee-6b38-74e3-9016-c618a2c822ee","chunk":"Read text file `README.md`.\n(2738 line(s) above)\n2739|with the negative `TestDecodeRefusesNonConptyBackendOnNativeWindows` legacy\n2740|refusal arm), three are\n2741|`partial` (Section 14.2 at 8/9, bound to\n2742|[`internal/cliresult`](internal/cliresult), whose undischarged clause `14.2#6`\n2743|is the process exit status this repository has no binary to produce; and\n2744|Section 15.1 at 5/7 and Section 15.3 at 2/3, both bound to\n2745|[`internal/axerror`](internal/axerror); the three undischarged clauses there are\n2746|the RPC hello obligation `15.1#5`, the bootstrap-row sentence `15.1#6` that\n2747|binds the provider plugin rather than the host, and the hello-key and\n2748|TerminalBackend-capability prohibition `15.3#3` - this repository builds no RPC\n2749|hello frame and no provider plugin, and advertises no Structured Error as a\n2750|TerminalBackend capability. The closed 16-capability admission registry in\n2751|[`internal/terminalbackend`](internal/terminalbackend) gates which operations\n2752|an admitted probe may confer; it is never advertised on an RPC hello path and\n2753|carries no Structured Error code, so the prohibition holds vacuously and the\n2754|clause stays undischarged. The same package carries the Section 4 lifecycle,\n2755|attach, entrypoint, replication, and historical-translation conformance\n2756|harness (`conformance.go`, exercised by `conformance_test.go`) and an\n2757|AST-derived refusal-arm inventory (`refusal_arm_inventory_test.go`) that\n2758|requires every production refusal arm to be declared with a resolving named\n2759|asserting test in both directions; behavioral proof stays with each row's\n2760|named test, which the inventory resolves textually),\n2761|one is\n2762|`sliver` (Section 10.3, whose chunk offset invariant is\n2763|enforced by `validateBlobDescriptor` while its two receiver clauses have no\n2764|implementation), three are `unmeasured` (Sections 7.3, 13.14.5 and 15.2, each of\n2765|which carries a gap saying why the scanner measures zero and what is missing),\n2766|and forty-eight are `unevidenced`. Twelve sections are recorded unowned.\n2767|All 13 sections added by v0.6.0 name pending task owners in the reviewed\n2768|registry gaps; these assignments grant no runtime admission. The\n2769|[adoption ownership map](internal/traceability/adoption-v0.6.0.md) separates\n2770|shared runtime APIs, their callers, product conformance and upstream source\n2771|publication validators, and preserves historical acceptance obligations.\n2772|Assigned-scope admission therefore succeeds today for `-section 6.2` and\n2773|nothing else; every other assignment is refused with its ratio and its gap.\n2774|A `partial` binding is refused by assigned-scope admission exactly like an\n2775|`unevidenced` one: admission requires `full`.\n2776|\n2777|One admitted binding out of fifty-six covers a single clause, and it is\n2778|disclosed here rather than hidden: without Section 6.2 the admit path would only\n(more lines below)","final_result":true}}}
