m inventory (`refusal_arm_inventory_test.go`) that\n2758|requires every production refusal arm to be declared with a resolving named\n2759|asserting test in both directions; behavioral proof stays with each row's\n2760|named test, which the inventory resolves textually),\n2761|one is\n2762|`sliver` (Section 10.3, whose chunk offset invariant is\n2763|enforced by `validateBlobDescriptor` while its two receiver clauses have no\n2764|implementation), three are `unmeasured` (Sections 7.3, 13.14.5 and 15.2, each of\n2765|which carries a gap saying why the scanner measures zero and what is missing),\n2766|and forty-eight are `unevidenced`. Twelve sections are recorded unowned.\n2767|All 13 sections added by v0.6.0 name pending task owners in the reviewed\n2768|registry gaps; these assignments grant no runtime admission. The\n2769|[adoption ownership map](internal/traceability/adoption-v0.6.0.md) separates\n2770|shared runtime APIs, their callers, product conformance and upstream source\n2771|publication validators, and preserves historical acceptance obligations.\n2772|Assigned-scope admission therefore succeeds today for `-section 6.2` and\n2773|nothing else; every other assignment is refused with its ratio and its gap.\n2774|A `partial` binding is refused by assigned-scope admission exactly like an\n2775|`unevidenced` one: admission requires `full`.\n2776|\n2777|One admitted binding out of fifty-six covers a single clause, and it is\n2778|disclosed here rather than hidden: without Section 6.2 the admit path would only\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
